Just that. If you need 1 decimal use 10 times larger numbers, if 2 then 
100 etc. But remember that computers use binary so multipliers like 16, 
128, etc. make more sense on computers.

Multiply first, divide last. Use double long intermediate results. 
Because of integer math FORTH has a special command for multiply and 
division '*/' that first multiplies two numbers and stores the product 
in double precision and then divides by the third and rounds to the 
original precision.

Something like this should do the trick:

long i,j,k,l;
/* set j, k and l somehow */
i = (long)((double) j * (double) k / l);

